-- The module whose `Basics` must survive. It writes no import at all, so `Int`
-- and `+` have to arrive implicitly.
--
-- The name matters. `Zed` sorts after `Aux`, and `Aux`'s only available entry is
-- the lower-priority `Maybe`. If the implicit edges are allocated importer by
-- importer in name order, `Aux -> Maybe` is added first, which puts `Zed` behind
-- `Maybe` behind `Aux` behind `Basics` — and the `Zed -> Basics` edge is then
-- refused as a loop, costing this module the import it actually needs. Allocating
-- them target by target in `DEFAULT_IMPORTS` order settles every `Basics` edge
-- before any `Maybe` edge can constrain the graph.
module Zed exposing (z)


z : Int
z = 1 + 2
